  <preface xml:id="preface-purpose">
    <title>Purpose and Audience</title>
  
    <p>
      This book is the working reference for the PROTEUS Short-Answer project. The
      project studies the mathematical justification visible in short student responses,
      develops a human coding procedure for representing that justification, implements
      an auditable computational workflow, and evaluates how well the resulting human and
      computational records support research.
    </p>
  
    <p>
      The book serves four connected purposes:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            introduce the research problem, theoretical motivation, analytic framework,
            and proposed validation study;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            define the canonical concepts and governing rules used to identify, represent,
            code, and review episodes of justification;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            train research assistants through a staged sequence of onboarding, blind
            extraction, codebook-assisted coding, calibration, supervised coding, and
            authorized production work; and
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            document the current computational pipeline while preserving the decisions,
            experiments, open questions, and version history needed to interpret the
            project as it develops.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      The principal audiences are undergraduate research assistants and collaborators 
      evaluating or extending the method. These readers do not need the same material 
      at the same time. The next preface provides reading paths and explains which 
      part of the book governs each type of decision.
    </p>
  
    <p>
      The project analyzes evidence visible in a response to a particular question. It
      does not treat one short response as a complete account of what a student knew,
      believed, considered, or could do in another setting. Human researchers remain
      responsible for the mathematical reference, episode interpretation, codebook,
      adjudication, and research claims.
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Computational outputs are therefore proposals and records for human review, not
      automatic research findings. A pipeline may organize responses, propose episode
      structures and code matches, record confidence, or route difficult cases. Those
      outputs must remain linked to the original evidence, governing versions, and any
      later human confirmation, revision, rejection, or unresolved status.
    </p>
  </preface>

  <preface xml:id="preface-how-to-use">
    <title>How to Use This Book</title>
  
    <p>
      Use the analytic chapters to understand the method, the handbook as the
      coder reference and milestone guide, and the glossary and appendices for 
      quick lookup.
    </p>
  
    <paragraphs xml:id="sec-book-architecture">
      <title>Division of Labor</title>
  
      <p>
        The book separates stable methodological content from milestone instructions,
        current artifact definitions, software documentation, and dated project records.
        Each kind of information has a primary home.
      </p>

      <p>Primary role of each part of the book:</p>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="28%"/>
        <col width="72%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Resource</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Primary purpose</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Project and Research Design</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Explain the research problem, intended contribution, corpus, comparison
              conditions, and validation logic.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Analytic Framework</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Define the theoretical boundary, Episode Model, codebook architecture,
              question-specific analytic frame, and normative mathematical reference.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Research Assistant Handbook</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              State the canonical coding workflow, edge-case and adjudication rules,
              worked examples, researcher-development principles, readiness competencies,
              and data-stewardship requirements.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Milestone Worksheets</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              State what a research assistant must read, may access, must complete,
              preserve, submit, and demonstrate during a particular milestone.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Computational Pipeline and Use</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Describe current software behavior, stage inputs and outputs, user actions,
              storage, provenance, and technical extension points.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Research Journal</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Preserve dated decisions, experiments, development findings, unresolved
              questions, and the roadmap without presenting historical material as a
              current instruction.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Appendices and Glossary</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Define stored fields, summarize the current protocol, identify current
              codebook and vocabulary artifacts, document migration and editorial history,
              and provide concise term lookup.
            </p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

    </paragraphs>
  
