    <paragraphs xml:id="sec-sources-authority">
      <title>FAQ</title>
  
      <p>
        Repeated warnings may appear where a rule becomes especially important, but the
        complete definition or procedure should have one authoritative home. Use the
        following map when locating or revising a rule.
      </p>

      <p>Authoritative source for common project questions:</p>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="31%"/>
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="35%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Question</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Authoritative source</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Supporting use elsewhere</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What claims may be made from a short response?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-theoretical-foundation" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Brief evidence-boundary reminders.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What is an episode, link status, relation, or correctness pair?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Procedural consequences and worksheet warnings.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>How are controls and question-specific normative links organized?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Current artifacts in the codebook appendix.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>How is an ordinary human coding pass performed?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Milestone-specific assignments and checklists.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>How are difficult cases, disagreements, and adjudication handled?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Worksheet comparison and escalation procedures.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What does defensible completed coding look like?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-worked-examples" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Parallel unsolved exercises in worksheets.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Why is training staged, and what counts as readiness?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-coding-methodology" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Milestone administration in Worksheets 1-6.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What privacy, access, provenance, and preservation rules apply?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Additional restrictions in the assigned worksheet.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What must a researcher do during the current milestone?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The assigned worksheet and packet.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Canonical definitions remain in the handbook.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What does each stored field mean?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Worksheets identify which fields are writable or withheld.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What are the current codebook and vocabulary artifacts?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="app-codebook-reference" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Packet manifests identify the versions used in a batch.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>How are human records used for validation?</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Operational coding remains in the handbook and worksheets.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>What does the current software do?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The Computational Pipeline and Use chapters.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Analytic definitions remain in the framework and handbook.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Why was a decision made or superseded?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The Research Journal and version history.</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Historical records do not govern current work by themselves.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

    </paragraphs>
  
    <paragraphs xml:id="sec-document-status">
      <title>Document Status and Version Discipline</title>
  
      <p>
        Status labels distinguish current authority from material retained for development
        or history. They describe the role of a section or artifact; version identifiers
        specify the exact form that governed a particular record.
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Canonical</title>
            <p>
              The current source of truth for the concept, rule, or procedure within its
              stated scope.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Provisional</title>
            <p>
              A proposed design or rule being developed or tested. It does not govern project
              work unless an assignment or study condition explicitly authorizes it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Historical</title>
            <p>
              A dated record preserved to explain how the project developed. It may support
              interpretation of earlier records but does not replace a current canonical rule.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Archived</title>
            <p>
              Superseded material retained for traceability or migration. It must not be used
              for current work unless a historical reconstruction specifically requires it.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
  
      <p>
        A status record should identify the effective date, the artifact or decision it
        supersedes, and the authority under which it was adopted. Earlier coding and pipeline
        runs remain tied to the versions that governed them; a later change does not silently
        reinterpret or overwrite those records.
      </p>
  
      <program language="text">
        Status: [Canonical | Provisional | Historical | Archived]
        Effective date: [YYYY-MM-DD]
        Supersedes: [identifier or none]
        Authority: [project decision, protocol, codebook, release, or condition]
      </program>
    </paragraphs>
  
